    return 0;
}
```
 This simulation demonstrates how wing angles oscillate throughout the downstroke and upstroke phases. The sinusoidal pattern reflects the natural motion that produces lift and thrust. Such mathematical models help ornithologists understand how bird flight differs from other vertebrate locomotion, revealing why these specialized birds occupy ecological niches unavailable to mammals.

When designing educational software that models material properties, developers often need to simulate how different substances behave in water. Consider a simple dissolution tracking system that monitors what happens when various materials contact liquid: 
```perl
my @materials = ("salt", "sand", "sugar", "gravel");
my %dissolves = (
    "salt" => 1,
    "sand" => 1,
    "sugar" => 1,
    "gravel" => 0
);
foreach my $material (@materials) {
    if ($dissolves{$material}) {
        print "$material dissolves in water\n";
    } else {
        print "$material does not dissolve\n";
    }
}
```
 This program represents how different materials respond when submerged. Salt, being ionic, breaks apart into ions and disperses throughout the water molecules, becoming invisible to the naked eye. Sand particles, similarly, distribute themselves evenly through water over time as the water molecules penetrate between individual grains. The key difference in solubility between these substances comes down to particle size and chemical composition. Understanding these dissolution patterns helps students grasp why some materials vanish into solutions while others settle to the bottom.

