public class DivisibilityChecker {
    public static boolean isDivisibleByThree(int number) {
        return number % 3 == 0;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isDivisibleByThree(12));
        System.out.println(isDivisibleByThree(11));
    }
}
```
 When this program executes, the first call returns true since 12 divides evenly by 3, yielding a remainder of zero. The second call confirms that 11 is divisible by three, which serves as a useful reminder that divisibility rules extend across the positive integers in consistent ways. Such computational verification helps students understand how modular arithmetic functions as a fundamental tool for testing these relationships between numbers.

Divisibility testing forms a cornerstone of computational number theory and integer validation. When working with large datasets, programmers often need to verify whether one number divides evenly into another. The following function demonstrates a practical approach to checking divisibility: 
```go
package main
import "fmt"
func isDivisible(n, divisor int) bool {
    return n%divisor == 0
}
func main() {
    testCases := []int{12, 11, 24, 33, 9, 15}
    for _, num := range testCases {
        if isDivisible(num, 3) {
            fmt.Printf("%d is divisible by 3\n", num)
        } else {
            fmt.Printf("%d is not divisible by 3\n", num)
        }
    }
}
```
