```perl
sub calculate_mercury_temperature {
    my ($solar_distance, $albedo, $time_of_day) = @_;
    my $solar_constant = 9126;
    my $received_energy = ($solar_constant / ($solar_distance ** 2)) * (1 - $albedo);
    my $dayside_temp = sqrt(sqrt($received_energy / 5.67e-8));
    my $nightside_temp = $dayside_temp * 0.15;
    my $current_temp = $time_of_day > 0.5 ? $dayside_temp : $nightside_temp;
    return int($current_temp - 273);
}
```
 This function accepts solar distance in AU, surface albedo values, and local time fraction, returning estimated surface temperature in Celsius. The stark temperature contrasts make Mercury a compelling subject for thermal dynamics research.
